---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const declare = `import { definePreference, oneOf } from "@skryensya/core/storage";

// The three things travel together: where you live, what you accept,
// and what is it when it is not there.
export const colorModePreference = definePreference({
  slot: "scheme",
  fallback: "system",
  parse: oneOf(["system", "light", "dark"]),
});`;

const vanilla = `import {
  getPreference,
  setPreference,
  clearPreference,
  subscribePreference,
} from "@skryensya/vanilla/storage";
import { colorModePreference } from "@skryensya/core/theme-toggle";

getPreference(colorModePreference);          // "system" when there is nothing
setPreference(colorModePreference, "dark");

// It is called now, and on each change, including that of another tab.
const off = subscribePreference(colorModePreference, (mode) => {
  document.documentElement.dataset.scheme = mode;
});
off();

// Forget ≠ save the default (see below).
clearPreference(colorModePreference);`;

const react = `import { useStoredPreference } from "@skryensya/react/storage";
import { colorModePreference } from "@skryensya/core/theme-toggle";

function ModeToggle() {
  const [mode, setMode, clear] = useStoredPreference(colorModePreference);

  return (
    <SegmentedControl value={mode} onValueChange={setMode}>
      {/* … */}
    </SegmentedControl>
  );
}`;

const bad = `// ❌ what there was, four times, with four copies of the try/catch
function load() {
  try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("sk") || "{}"); }
  catch { return {}; }
}
function save(patch) {
  localStorage.setItem("sk", JSON.stringify({ ...load(), ...patch }));
}
const value = load().scheme;
if (value === "light" || value === "dark") { /* … */ }`;

const good = `// ✅ the same, with the slot and the save in the declaration
import { getPreference } from "@skryensya/vanilla/storage";
import { colorModePreference } from "./preferences";

const value = getPreference(colorModePreference);`;

const entry = `// A single entry, a single object. Ayes is seen in devtools:
localStorage["sk"] === JSON.stringify({
  scheme: "dark",
  contrast: "high",
  binding: "react",
  screen: "mobile",
  palette: "verde",
});

// And that is why "reset the site" is one line:
localStorage.removeItem("sk");`;

const prepaint = `---
// In the frontmatter: the names come out of the declarations.
const PREPAINT = {
  key: STORAGE_KEY,
  scheme: colorModePreference.slot,
};
---
<script is:inline define:vars={{ PREPAINT }}>
  var s = {};
  try { s = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(PREPAINT.key) || "{}"); } catch (e) {}
  if (s[PREPAINT.scheme] === "dark") { /* … */ }
</script>`;
---

<Base
  title="Almacenamiento"
  documentLayout="grid"
  description="The only way to save preferences: one entry, declared preferences, and readings that never fail."
>
  <h1>Storage</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A design system saves preferences: color mode, contrast, density, whatever the product adds. It is not a component, it is plumbing that every consumer reinvents, and that this repo had reinvented <strong>four times</strong>, each copy with its own <code>try/catch</code>, its own key, and its own handwritten save. So it ships the way any behaviour with no platform equivalent ships here ( <a href="https://github.com/Skryensya/skryensya-ui/blob/main/docs/decisions">decision 14</a> ): the pure codec in <code>@skryensya/core/storage</code>, the imperative binding in <code>@skryensya/vanilla/storage</code>, and the hook in <code>@skryensya/react/storage</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Three rules</h2>
  <p>Those three are the whole design. Everything else follows from them.</p>

  <h3>1. One entry</h3>
  <p>
    Everything lives under <strong>one</strong> <code>localStorage</code> key holding <strong>one</strong> JSON object. Not one key per preference: a scattering of loose <code>sk-*</code> keys cannot be cleared, inspected or versioned, which is exactly what <code>sk-playground</code> was turning into. With one entry, "reset this site" is a single <code>removeItem</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={entry} lang="ts" />

  <h3>2. A preference is declared, never ad hoc</h3>
  <p>
    <code>definePreference</code> asks for a slot, a fallback and a <strong>parser</strong>, and the parser is not optional. What is stored is <em>untrusted input</em>: the reader can edit it, and a version you shipped last month can leave a shape behind that today's code has never seen. Every call site this replaced was already hand-writing its own <code>isBinding</code> or <code>isColorMode</code> beside the read. Putting the guard in the declaration is what <em>removes</em> that duplication rather than relocating it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={declare} lang="ts" />

  <h3>3. Reading never fails</h3>
  <p>
    An unreadable store, a value that parses to nothing, a <code>localStorage</code> that <strong>throws</strong> because the reader is in private mode: all three return the fallback. Watch the third one, because it is the case that breaks in production and never on your machine. A blocked <code>localStorage</code> does not "return null", it <em>throws on property access</em>, so a bare <code>localStorage.getItem</code> at module level takes the whole page down for those readers. Here every access is guarded, and an unreachable store degrades to memory: the preference stops persisting and nothing else changes.
  </p>

  <h2>The before and after</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={bad} lang="ts" />
  <CodeBlock code={good} lang="ts" />

  <h2>Vanilla</h2>
  <p>
    <code>subscribePreference</code> is what earns the module its place, beyond saving a few lines: it calls back <strong>immediately</strong> with the current value, and again on every change, including one made in <strong>another tab</strong>. None of the hand-written call sites subscribed to the <code>storage</code> event, so two open tabs drifted apart until you reloaded. That is the difference between a preference and a per-tab variable.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={vanilla} lang="ts" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    <code>useStoredPreference</code> is <code>useState</code> that is remembered. Like <code>useHotkey</code>, it relies on core and never on the vanilla package: the two bindings are siblings on the same contract, so a React app installs one and a vanilla app the other.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={react} lang="tsx" />
  <p>
    Use <code>useSyncExternalStore</code>, and not <code>useState</code> + <code>useEffect</code>, for two non-style reasons. <code>localStorage</code> is an external <em>store</em>: reading it during rendering is an effect that <em>rips</em> with concurrent rendering, two components reading the same preference in one pass can disagree. And it gives a separate path for SSR: <code>getServerSnapshot</code> returns the fallback, so the server render and the client's first paint agree instead of hydrating into a mismatch the moment the saved value differs from the default.
  </p>

  <h2>Forgetting is not saving the default</h2>
  <p>
    <code>clearPreference</code> exists apart from <code>setPreference(pref, fallback)</code> on purpose. Saving the default freezes <em>today's default</em> in the reader's browser: if the product changes that default later, it never reaches that person. Forgetting leaves them following the system.
  </p>

  <h2>The only exception: before the first paint</h2>
  <p>
    A script that runs <strong>before any</strong> module (the one that prevents theme flash) cannot import the primitive: importing is exactly what exists to happen after. That script touches <code>localStorage</code> directly, and is the only place in the repo that does so.
  </p>
  <p>
    What <strong>no</strong> does is rewrite the key or the slot names: it passes them to you
    the frontmatter from the actual declarations, so renaming a slot cannot leave the script
    reading a slot that no one writes anymore. What is duplicated there are the value guards,
    because a parser is a function and functions do not survive serialization. That is the known cost of the exception, and the only one.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={prepaint} lang="html" />

  <h2>Where</h2>
  <p>
    Preferences are declared next to the module that owns the <em>type</em>. <code>colorModePreference</code> lives with ThemeToggle, and ComponentPreview's two live with ComponentPreview: the slot name and its guard are part of the contract a second consumer would have to match, and a slot retyped in two apps is a slot that will differ in one of them. What belongs to the product (a docs palette is a tenant's business) is declared by the app: on this site, together in <code>src/lib/preferences.ts</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That rule is what makes this checkable: grep for <code>localStorage</code> outside those files and the only hit should be the prepaint script.
  </p>
</Base>
